---
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import FaqSection from "../components/FaqSection.astro";
import InlineCode from "../components/InlineCode.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import GetStartedDialog from "../components/GetStartedDialog.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import SetupSteps from "../components/SetupSteps.astro";
import VsCard from "../components/VsCard.astro";
import {
  COMPARISON_ROWS,
  faqPageStructuredData,
  OPENCLAW_DAY_TO_DAY_PARAGRAPHS,
  OPENCLAW_DESCRIPTION,
  OPENCLAW_DOCS_URL,
  OPENCLAW_FAQS,
  OPENCLAW_FEATURES_URL,
  OPENCLAW_GETTING_STARTED_URL,
  OPENCLAW_H1,
  OPENCLAW_GITHUB_URL,
  OPENCLAW_RELATED,
  OPENCLAW_SANDBOX_URL,
  OPENCLAW_SETUP_PARAGRAPHS,
  OPENCLAW_TITLE,
  OPENCLAW_UPDATED,
} from "../guide";
import { fetchGithubStars, formatStarCount } from "../github";
import { getHomeCopy } from "../i18n/home";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { ogPublicPath } from "../og-pages";
import { GITHUB_URL, SELF_HOST_GUIDE_PATH } from "../site";
import { vsCard } from "../vs-points";

const home = getHomeCopy("en");
const stars = await fetchGithubStars();
const starsLabel = stars === null ? "Star" : formatStarCount(stars);
const structuredData = faqPageStructuredData(OPENCLAW_FAQS);
const card = vsCard("openclaw-alternative");
---

<BaseLayout
  title={OPENCLAW_TITLE}
  description={OPENCLAW_DESCRIPTION}
  {structuredData}
  localeAlternates={false}
  ogImage={ogPublicPath("openclaw-alternative")}
>
  <div class="container">
    <Header starsLabel={starsLabel} />
    <GetStartedDialog copy={home.getStartedDialog} waitlist={home.waitlist} />
    <main id="main" class="privacy-copy guide">
      <h1>{OPENCLAW_H1}</h1>
      <p class="alt-note">Updated {OPENCLAW_UPDATED}.</p>
      {
        card && (
          <VsCard otherName="OpenClaw" rakazo={card.rakazo} other={card.other} />
        )
      }
      <SetupSteps />
      <p>
        The main difference is simplicity. Like Grok Bot, Rakazo is just chat: you set up the bot
        and manage it from that chat. OpenClaw is also
        open source and self-hosted. Its{" "}
        <a href={OPENCLAW_GETTING_STARTED_URL}>getting-started guide</a> is a longer path: a CLI
        installer, an onboarding wizard, a Gateway you run in the terminal or install as a background
        service, and a config file.
      </p>
      <p>
        They are not the same product, and Rakazo does not import an OpenClaw setup. OpenClaw details
        below follow <a href={OPENCLAW_DOCS_URL}>OpenClaw's documentation</a>. Rakazo details follow
        this project. Both change. The linked docs are the place to confirm a detail.
      </p>

      <h2>Setup</h2>
      {OPENCLAW_SETUP_PARAGRAPHS.map((paragraph) => (
        <p>
          <InlineCode text={paragraph} />
        </p>
      ))}

      <h2>Day-to-day management</h2>
      {OPENCLAW_DAY_TO_DAY_PARAGRAPHS.map((paragraph) => (
        <p>
          <InlineCode text={paragraph} />
        </p>
      ))}

      <h2>Comparison</h2>
      <div class="guide-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Rakazo and OpenClaw, from each project's public documentation.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Topic</th>
              <th scope="col">Rakazo</th>
              <th scope="col">OpenClaw</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              COMPARISON_ROWS.map((row) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{row.aspect}</th>
                  <td><InlineCode text={row.rakazo} /></td>
                  <td><InlineCode text={row.openclaw} /></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Key differences</h2>
      <h3>Teammates in an app, or an assistant in your chats</h3>
      <p>
        Rakazo is a web, desktop, and mobile app. A bot has a job, a computer, and routines saved as
        Markdown you can read and edit. OpenClaw is a Gateway you message from the chat apps you
        already use, with a browser Control UI and mobile nodes around that gateway.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rakazo can attach optional Slack, WhatsApp, Telegram, iMessage (Sendblue), and Feishu/Lark
        connectors. Those are extras. Chat apps are OpenClaw's main surface, including Discord, Signal,
        iMessage, Slack, Telegram, WhatsApp, and other channel plugins.
      </p>
      <h3>Where the computer runs</h3>
      <p>
        Rakazo runs browser and shell work on a computer. Local Docker is the default. E2B, Daytona,
        CreateOS, and Box are optional remote computers. An explicit desktop provider can run on the
        host, and it is not the default.
      </p>
      <p>
        OpenClaw can sandbox tool execution. Its docs say sandboxing is off by default, and that the
        Gateway process stays on the host. Documented sandbox backends include Docker, Podman, SSH,
        OpenShell, and Crabbox.
      </p>
      <h3>Models and what you pay</h3>
      <p>
        Both let you bring a model provider, including a server you host. Rakazo connects OpenAI,
        Anthropic, Google, OpenRouter, Vercel AI Gateway, an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, or a
        local model server, and each bot can use a different model. OpenClaw documents Anthropic,
        OpenAI, Google, and others, including self-hosted OpenAI-compatible and Anthropic-compatible
        endpoints, with failover.
      </p>
      <p>
        Neither project charges a seat fee for the software. OpenClaw's docs say it has no paid tier
        and no hosted service. Rakazo is free to self-host. Hosted Rakazo Cloud is not generally
        available. You pay for the models, machines, and optional computer providers you use.
      </p>
      <h3>License</h3>
      <p>
        Rakazo is Apache-2.0, maintained by Inbox Zero Inc. OpenClaw is MIT, stewarded by the OpenClaw
        Foundation, which the project describes as an independent 501(c)(3).
      </p>

      <h2>Which shape fits</h2>
      <h3>Rakazo</h3>
      <p>
        Repeated browser and shell work, scheduled operational routines, an audit log, and approval
        boundaries, with the same bots on the web, desktop, and mobile clients. <a href={SELF_HOST_GUIDE_PATH}>Self-host it from the guide</a>.
      </p>
      <h3>OpenClaw</h3>
      <p>
        An assistant that lives in chat apps you already use, with channel plugins, skills, and a
        gateway on your machine. Start from the <a href={OPENCLAW_DOCS_URL}>OpenClaw docs</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul class="source-list">
        <li><a href={OPENCLAW_DOCS_URL}>OpenClaw docs</a></li>
        <li><a href={OPENCLAW_GETTING_STARTED_URL}>OpenClaw getting started</a></li>
        <li><a href={OPENCLAW_FEATURES_URL}>OpenClaw features</a></li>
        <li><a href={OPENCLAW_SANDBOX_URL}>OpenClaw sandboxing</a></li>
        <li><a href={OPENCLAW_GITHUB_URL}>OpenClaw source</a></li>
        <li><a href={SELF_HOST_GUIDE_PATH}>Rakazo self-host guide</a></li>
        <li><a href={GITHUB_URL}>Rakazo source</a></li>
      </ul>

      <FaqSection items={OPENCLAW_FAQS} />

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/">Rakazo home</a></li>
        <li><a href={SELF_HOST_GUIDE_PATH}>Self-hosted AI agent guide</a></li>
        {OPENCLAW_RELATED.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2 id="selfhost">Get started</h2>
      <p>Self-host Rakazo on your machine, or leave an email for hosted Rakazo Cloud.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <Button href={SELF_HOST_GUIDE_PATH}>Self-host guide</Button>
        <button class="button button--primary" type="button" data-get-started-open>Get started</button>
        <Button href={GITHUB_URL} variant="secondary" external>View on GitHub</Button>
      </div>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
